# transfer.topic -- sendability and the cross-VM value transfer
# (SENDABILITY-AND-VALUE-COPY-MILESTONE-1.md): the concept and its
# diagnostics, one topic per code.

@topic concept.sendability
title: Sendable values and std::transfer
summary: A sendable value (the intrinsic trait Sendable) can be rebuilt in another VM: std::transfer::encode writes it into owned bytes, std::transfer::decode[T] rebuilds it in this VM.
index: concepts
alias: sendable; Sendable; send a value to another VM; copy a value between VMs; serialize for another thread; transfer; value copy; cross-VM message
keywords: Sendable, sendable, std::transfer, encode, decode, transfer, copy, VM, heap, NOT-SENDABLE, TRANSFER-OPEN-TYPE, SENDABLE-DECLARATION, sendable clause, opaque, through, TransferMalformed, TransferTypeMismatch
related: error.not-sendable error.transfer-open-type error.sendable-declaration concept.opaque-structs concept.affine concept.contexts

== WHAT IT IS
A Botlish VM owns its heap, its runtime resources (coroutine stacks,
event registrations, mailboxes) and its installed contexts. A value is
*sendable* when another VM can reconstruct an equivalent value from its
content alone. The compiler derives this from the value's static type;
the intrinsic trait `Sendable` names it, like `Hashable`: a struct slot may
require it (`struct Envelope[T satisfies Sendable]:`).

`std::transfer::encode(value)` writes a sendable value into a
`std::abi::bytes::Bytes` that holds no address and no number of this
compilation's making; `std::transfer::decode[T](data)` reads such bytes
into a value allocated in this VM. The bytes carry a fingerprint of T's
schema: bytes of another type are refused (TransferTypeMismatch), and every
value is checked against T as it is read.

== RULES
  * Sendable: unit, bool, Int (any size; an integer domain's range is
    checked by the receiver), UnicodeChar, Float64, DecimalRounded, raw C
    floating values, str; Lists, ImmutableSets, MutableVectors and
    MutableArrays of sendable elements; structs, anonymous structs and tree
    structs of sendable fields; enum cases; and an opaque struct whose
    owning module states how it is rebuilt (its `sendable` clause:
    std::abi::bytes::Bytes and MutableBytes, std::mutable_map::MutableMap,
    std::mutable_set::MutableSet and std::immutable_map::ImmutableMap state
    it).
  * Not sendable: functions and closures, coroutine handles, C pointers and
    native views or edits, context structs (authority), refinements (their
    proof is this program's), an opaque struct whose owner states nothing
    (std::event_loop::Reg, std::actor::Address: identities of one VM), and
    `any` or an unconstrained imprint variable (the type does not say).
  * A transfer's schema is its static type, closed where the transfer is
    compiled. A generic body transferring a value of a variable its
    signature constrains to Sendable (`Box[T]` of `struct Box[T satisfies
    Sendable]`) is compiled once per binding of the variable, each copy
    writing its closed schema; a value seen through a `Sendable` trait view
    is encoded the same way (its function cloned per witness type). decode's
    type is a concrete type, never a trait view (TRANSFER-OPEN-TYPE).
  * A copy is a new value: mutating a received MutableVector or MutableMap
    changes nothing in the sender, and the reverse. A map's or set's index
    and stored hashes are computed again by the receiver.
  * Sending carries no authority: a decoded value is data; the receiver's
    contexts are its own.
  * std::actor's messages between actors of one VM are ordinary values and
    need not be sendable.

@example accept
import std::transfer
import std::abi::bytes
import std::mutable_vector

struct Job:
    id: int
    tags: MutableVector[str]

job = Job {id: 7, tags: std::mutable_vector::from_list(["new"])}
copy = std::transfer::decode[Job](std::transfer::encode(job)):
    on TransferMalformed:
        job
    on TransferVersion:
        job
    on TransferTypeMismatch:
        job
    on TransferInvalidValue:
        job
    on TransferLimit:
        job
copy.tags.push("copied")
[copy.id, std::mutable_vector::length(copy.tags), std::mutable_vector::length(job.tags)]
@caption A copy is a new value: pushing onto the copy's vector leaves the original's alone
@value [7, 2, 1]
@end

@topic error.not-sendable
title: NOT-SENDABLE: a value that cannot be rebuilt in another VM
summary: A transferred type holds what no other VM can rebuild (a function, coroutine, C pointer, context, unstated opaque struct) or does not say what its values are (any).
index: errors
alias: not sendable; cannot send a function; cannot send a context; value cannot cross VMs; Sendable not satisfied
keywords: Sendable, sendable, transfer, encode, decode, function, closure, coroutine, pointer, context, opaque, any, reason path
related: concept.sendability error.sendable-declaration error.transfer-open-type
code: CORE SEMANTIC NOT-SENDABLE

== MEANING
A transfer copies a value's content into bytes another VM rebuilds it
from. Some values have no such content: a function is code and the
environment it captured, a coroutine handle owns a suspended computation, a
C pointer addresses memory only its owner understands, a context struct is
installed authority, and an opaque struct's fields may be an identity of
one VM (a slot and a generation of its event loop) rather than a value. A
value whose static type is `any` may be any of these. The message names the
offending component and the path to it ("field \"run\" of Job (Fn{...}):
...").

== WHAT TO DO
Send the data the receiver needs instead of the resource: a request
describing the work rather than a closure, the name or number the receiver
maps to its own context or registration. Give a value of `any` a declared
type. For an opaque struct of your own module whose fields are its value,
state it: `sendable fields`.

@example reject
import std::transfer
import std::abi::bytes

struct Job:
    id: int
    run: Fn{args: [int], return: int}

fn send(job: Job) -> std::abi::bytes::Bytes:
    std::transfer::encode(job)

fn ident(x: int) -> int:
    x

send(Job {id: 1, run: ident})
@caption A struct holding a function is not sendable
@error CORE SEMANTIC NOT-SENDABLE
@end

@example reject
import std::transfer
import std::abi::bytes
import std::event_loop

fn send(r: std::event_loop::Reg) -> std::abi::bytes::Bytes:
    std::transfer::encode(r)

1
@caption A registration is an identity of one VM's event loop: its owner does not declare it sendable
@error CORE SEMANTIC NOT-SENDABLE
@end

@topic error.transfer-open-type
title: TRANSFER-OPEN-TYPE: a transfer whose type is not known where it is written
summary: A transfer's static type does not name its values' type: decode of a trait view (decode[Sendable]), whose bytes could be of any witness; the transfer's schema is its static type.
index: errors
alias: transfer in a generic function; encode of an imprint variable; open type; decode a trait view
keywords: transfer, encode, decode, imprint variable, generic, schema, Sendable, satisfies, trait view
related: concept.sendability error.not-sendable
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRANSFER-OPEN-TYPE

== MEANING
The bytes a transfer writes describe the value's static type (its schema
and fingerprint), which the compiler writes into the call. Inside a
generic body whose parameter is `Box[T]` with `T satisfies Sendable`, the
schema depends on the caller's T: the compiler compiles such a body once
per binding of T (as it clones a trait-polymorphic function per witness),
so each copy encodes and decodes a closed type. What stays open is a
transfer whose type names no type at all: `decode[Sendable]` would rebuild
a value of whichever witness wrote the bytes.

== WHAT TO DO
Decode as the concrete type the bytes were written as (`decode[Job]`);
to accept several, send an enum or a struct that says which.

@example accept
import std::transfer
import std::abi::bytes

struct Box[T satisfies Sendable]:
    value: T

fn seal(b: Box[T]) -> std::abi::bytes::Bytes:
    std::transfer::encode(b)

seal(Box[int] {value: 1}).length().value > 0
@caption A generic encode of a Sendable-constrained slot: compiled per binding of T
@value true
@end

@example reject
import std::transfer
import std::abi::bytes

fn open(data: std::abi::bytes::Bytes) -> Sendable errors TransferMalformed, TransferVersion, TransferTypeMismatch, TransferInvalidValue, TransferLimit:
    std::transfer::decode[Sendable](data)

1
@caption decode of a trait view: the bytes could be of any witness
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRANSFER-OPEN-TYPE
@end

@topic error.sendable-declaration
title: SENDABLE-DECLARATION: an opaque struct's sendable clause does not hold
summary: Only an opaque, non-context, non-resource struct states `sendable fields` or `sendable through(TO, FROM) as R`; its parts must be sendable and TO and FROM total, context-free functions.
index: errors
alias: sendable clause; sendable fields; sendable through; owner statement of sendability
keywords: sendable, opaque, owner, fields, through, representation, projection, rebuild, context, resource, any
related: concept.sendability error.not-sendable concept.opaque-structs
code: CORE SEMANTIC SENDABLE-DECLARATION

== MEANING
The compiler cannot tell from an opaque struct's fields what they mean, so
only its owner may state how a copy in another VM is rebuilt:

  * `sendable fields`: a field-wise copy is the value; every field must be
    sendable (an `any` field is not: the statement would be a marker on a
    representation the copy cannot rebuild).
  * `sendable through(TO, FROM) as R`: TO (a function of the module taking
    the struct, returning R) projects a value to R, which is copied, and
    FROM (taking R, returning the struct) rebuilds it in the receiver: for a
    representation with derived state, such as a hash table's index. R must
    be sendable; TO and FROM must declare no error, require no context and
    never yield.
  * `bytes(F)`, `mutable_bytes(F)`: the standard library's byte storage.

A transparent struct needs no clause (its fields decide), and a context or
resource struct can never be sendable.

@example reject
import std::transfer

opaque struct Handle:
    raw: any
    sendable fields

1
@caption An any field makes no statement the copy can keep
@error CORE SEMANTIC SENDABLE-DECLARATION
@end

@example accept
import std::transfer
import std::abi::bytes

opaque struct Money:
    cents: int
    sendable fields

m = Money {cents: 250}
back = std::transfer::decode[Money](std::transfer::encode(m)):
    on TransferMalformed:
        m
    on TransferVersion:
        m
    on TransferTypeMismatch:
        m
    on TransferInvalidValue:
        m
    on TransferLimit:
        m
back == m
@caption The owner states that a field-wise copy is the value
@value true
@end
